Recursive Approach for Linked List Problems
Overview

The recursive approach for linked list problems is based on the idea that each node’s
result depends on the result of the remaining list.
Instead of iterating through nodes, recursion processes the list from end to start,
rebuilding links during backtracking.

Core Idea
At every recursive call:
Solve the problem for head.next
Decide whether to keep or remove the current node
Return the new head of the processed list

General Recursive Pattern
ListNode solve(ListNode head) {
    if (head == null) return null;   // Base case
    head.next = solve(head.next);    // Recursive call
    // Decision on current node
    if (condition) {
        return head.next;            // Skip current node
    }
    return head;                     // Keep current node
}

Example: Remove Elements from Linked List
public ListNode removeElements(ListNode head, int val) {
    if (head == null) return null;
    head.next = removeElements(head.next, val);
    return head.val == val ? head.next : head;
}

Step-by-Step Explanation
Recursion continues until the end of the list (head == null)
During backtracking:
Each node checks whether it should be removed
Links are rebuilt automatically
The final returned node becomes the new head of the list

Time & Space Complexity
Complexity Type	Value
Time Complexity	O(n)
Space Complexity	O(n) (recursive call stack)

Note: This approach is not space-optimal due to recursion stack usage.

When to Use Recursion

✅ Clean and readable logic
✅ Useful for conceptual clarity
❌ Not suitable for very large lists (risk of stack overflow)
❌ Avoid when strict O(1) space is required

Recursive Linked List Checklist

 Base case handled (head == null)
 Recursive call made on head.next
 head.next properly reconnected
 Correct return (head vs head.next)
 Aware of extra stack space usage

Key Takeaway

The recursive linked list approach trades space efficiency for code clarity,
making it ideal for learning, interviews, and problems where readability is prioritized.